-- title:
--   Lemma 52 — stability of the nearby sequence for the data structures
-- statement:
--   In the setting of Lemma 51, let $W^{(k)}=T(x^{(k)})^{1/2-1/p}\widetilde C(x^{(k)})$, $\widehat W^{(k)}=T(\hat x^{(k)})^{1/2-1/p}\widetilde C(\hat x^{(k)})$ and $\hat\tau^{(k)}=\tau(\hat x^{(k)})$. There is one sequence $\hat x^{(k)}$ for which, with high probability, the three bullets of Lemma 51 hold together with
--
--   1. $\max_i\|(\widehat W_i^{(k)})^{-1}(W_i^{(k)}-\widehat W_i^{(k)})\|_{\mathrm{op}}\lesssim\alpha_{\mathrm{nb}}$;
--   2. $\|T(\hat x^{(k)})^{-1}(\hat\tau^{(k+1)}-\hat\tau^{(k)})\|_{\hat\tau^{(k)}}\lesssim\beta/C_{\mathrm{norm}}$;
--   3. $\bigl\|\bigl(\|(\widehat W_i^{(k)})^{-1}(\widehat W_i^{(k+1)}-\widehat W_i^{(k)})\|_{\mathrm{op}}\bigr)_i\bigr\|_{\hat\tau^{(k)}}\lesssim\beta/C_{\mathrm{norm}}$.
--
--   These are the stability conditions required by the Lewis-weight and inverse maintenance data structures.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - reading: constants hidden in ≲ and O(·), C "sufficiently large" (p.10), C_Var "sufficiently large" (Definition 19) → ∃ K₀ C₀ for each bound N on block sizes, ∀ C ≥ C₀, ∃ a C_Var threshold depending on C only, besides C_Var ≥ C_norm; reason: §3.1 puts C above the hidden constants, Algorithm 1 fixes β without n_i, n_i β ≲ 1 (p.24) needs N, and Definition 19 asks C_Var ≥ C_norm and "a sufficiently large constant"
--   - reading: A any matrix → A of full column rank; reason: P(·) and (A⊤T⁻¹∇²Φ⁻¹A)⁻¹ are defined on p.10 for full column rank
--   - encoding: m unrestricted → m ≥ 2; reason: θ = 1/(10 log m) is undefined at m = 1; this only excludes an undefined case
--   - reading: x̂ not defined on p.45 → ∃ x̂ with x̂^(0) = x^(0), a function of the sample history; reason: the proof constructs it (pp.91–92)
--   - reading: "the sequence generated by repeatedly calling Algorithm 1" → a run: each input depends adaptively on earlier samples, with prescribed conditional laws of R; reason: adaptive x̄, τ̄, µ̄, H̄ are allowed by the paper
--   - reading: w.h.p., with ϵ-centering standing (§4.2) → failure ≤ m⁻¹⁰ jointly over k, on the event that all points are centered and all samples are in the good event of Definition 19 items 4, 5; reason: those items fail with probability m⁻¹⁰ per call, so T calls cannot be m⁻¹⁰ unconditionally
--   - encoding: k ∈ [T] → calls 0, …, T − 1 and points 0, …, T; reason: an index shift
--   - reading: "Suppose the setting of Lemma 51" → one x̂ with the bullets of Lemma 51 and Lemma 52 on one event; reason: an independent ∃ would be a weaker lemma
-- source:
--   Zhang, Wright, A Fast Lewis-weight Interior Point Method for Linear Optimization over Product-Domain (manuscript, sha256 66072000…), Lemma 52, p.45

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_BlockLewisIPM_ShortStep

open Matrix MeasureTheory ProbabilityTheory

namespace BlockLewisIPM

open Instance

/-- **Lemma 52**, p.45. Zhang, Wright, *A Fast Lewis-weight Interior Point Method for Linear Optimization
over Product-Domain* (manuscript, sha256 66072000…).

**Formalization Note.** "Suppose the setting of Lemma 51": the same `x̂`, so the theorem states the bullets
of Lemma 51 and of Lemma 52 for one `x̂` on one event. `C̃(x)` is `C̃(x, ∇²Φ(x))`. `W` is not symmetric and the
norm is the plain operator norm of the printed product. `K₀` is the constant of `≲`. See `lemma_51`. -/
theorem lemma_52 (N : ℕ) : ∃ K₀ C₀ : ℝ, 0 < K₀ ∧ 10000 ≤ C₀ ∧ ∀ C ≥ C₀, ∃ Cv₀ : ℝ,
    ∀ {ι : Type} [Fintype ι] [DecidableEq ι] (I : Instance ι), I.Standing N →
    ∀ Cvar ≥ Cv₀,
    let P := I.par C
    ∀ αnb : ℝ, 0 < αnb → αnb ≤ P.β → P.β / αnb ≤ Cvar ^ 2 →
    ∀ (T : ℕ) (U : Run I), IsRun P Cvar U →
    ∃ xh : ∀ k, History ι k → I.Vec, (∀ h, xh 0 h = (U.step 0 h).x) ∧
      ∀ {Ω : Type} [MeasurableSpace Ω] (μ : Measure Ω) [IsProbabilityMeasure μ]
        (R : ℕ → Ω → ι → ℝ), Realizes U μ R →
        μ {ω | RunGood P Cvar U R T ω ∧
            ¬ (NearbyBullets P αnb U xh R T ω ∧ StabilityBullets P K₀ αnb U xh R T ω)} ≤
          ENNReal.ofReal ((P.m : ℝ) ^ (-10 : ℝ)) := by sorry

end BlockLewisIPM
